#### 4.X Hyperparameter Sensitivity Analysis

To understand the impact of individual hyperparameters on CorVAE's downstream performance, we perform a one-at-a-time (OAT) sensitivity analysis on the Shoppers dataset. Starting from the optimal configuration found by Bayesian optimization (Optuna, 30 trials), we systematically vary each hyperparameter while holding all others at their optimum. For each configuration, we train the VAE for 700 epochs, distill with K-Means at IPC=50, and evaluate via XGBoost ROC-AUC on a held-out test set.

##### Parameters Under Study

| Parameter | Symbol | Optimal Value | Range Studied |
|-----------|--------|---------------|---------------|
| Maximum KL weight | $\beta_{\max}$ | 0.0077 | $\{10^{-4}, 10^{-3}, 5\times10^{-3}, \mathbf{0.0077}, 0.01, 0.05, 0.1\}$ |
| Minimum KL weight | $\beta_{\min}$ | $1.4\times10^{-4}$ | $\{10^{-7}, 10^{-6}, 10^{-5}, \mathbf{1.4\times10^{-4}}, 10^{-3}\}$ |
| Transformer layers | $L$ | 1 | $\{1, 2, 3, 4\}$ |
| Attention heads | $H$ | 2 | $\{1, 2, 4, 8\}$ |
| Token dimension | $d$ | 8 | $\{4, 8, 16, 32\}$ |
| FFN expansion factor | $f$ | 16 | $\{4, 8, 16, 32, 64\}$ |
| Learning rate | $\eta$ | 0.005 | $\{10^{-4}, 5\times10^{-4}, 10^{-3}, \mathbf{0.005}, 0.01\}$ |
| Annealing coefficient | $\lambda$ | 0.9 | $\{0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9\}$ |

##### Interpretation

The KL weight schedule follows a cyclical annealing scheme controlled by $\beta_{\min}$, $\beta_{\max}$, and $\lambda$. During training, $\beta$ ramps from $\beta_{\min}$ to $\beta_{\max}$ over each cycle, with $\lambda$ governing the proportion of each cycle spent in the ramp-up phase vs. the plateau. This provides a principled balance between reconstruction quality and latent space regularity.

